WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.126 --> 00:00:04.003 line:68%
Here at Yale we're committed
to improving the world today

00:00:04.003 --> 00:00:06.965 line:68%
and for future generations
through outstanding research

00:00:06.965 --> 00:00:10.802 line:68%
and scholarship, education,
preservation and practice.

00:00:11.553 --> 00:00:14.889
Generations of generous
alumni and friends have,

00:00:14.889 --> 00:00:16.683
through their financial support,

00:00:16.683 --> 00:00:19.060
ensured that we can fulfill
this commitment.

00:00:19.769 --> 00:00:23.815
Helping us to sustain current
programing, fund new ambitions,

00:00:24.315 --> 00:00:26.818
and guarantee
that future generations

00:00:26.818 --> 00:00:29.487
may pursue excellence
just as we do today.

00:00:30.613 --> 00:00:33.491
Our donors' generosity
established Yale's endowment,

00:00:34.075 --> 00:00:37.454
which with careful stewardship,
provides a long term

00:00:37.454 --> 00:00:39.831
and stable source of revenue
for the university

00:00:40.248 --> 00:00:43.168
and underpins the sustainability
of Yale's mission

00:00:43.626 --> 00:00:46.254
and the fulfillment
of our strategic vision.

00:00:47.589 --> 00:00:50.550
This video will explain
how the endowment works,

00:00:51.009 --> 00:00:53.595
what restrictions are placed
on its use,

00:00:53.595 --> 00:00:57.182
and its impact
on the university and beyond.

00:00:58.808 --> 00:01:00.852
[reflective music]

00:01:00.852 --> 00:01:02.020
(Scott)
The university endowment

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:05.106
is made up of almost 8000
different accounts.

00:01:05.148 --> 00:01:06.399
These are contributions

00:01:06.399 --> 00:01:08.818 line:68%
that have been made
by individuals over the decades,

00:01:09.402 --> 00:01:12.697 line:68%
and some of them
are restricted in their use

00:01:12.697 --> 00:01:16.075 line:68%
and that the donor had
a particular interest in mind of

00:01:16.075 --> 00:01:18.328
how they wanted
to support the university

00:01:18.328 --> 00:01:19.913
That might be for a scholarship.

00:01:19.913 --> 00:01:22.540
It might be for a particular
unit or department.

00:01:22.916 --> 00:01:25.126
And so these would be restricted
endowments,

00:01:25.627 --> 00:01:29.172
and about 75% of the total
university endowment

00:01:29.172 --> 00:01:31.216
is under these
restricted accounts.

00:01:31.424 --> 00:01:34.093
So we have an obligation
as a university

00:01:34.302 --> 00:01:37.639
to utilize the funds
in a way consistent with how the

00:01:38.223 --> 00:01:40.809
the donor
made their contribution.

00:01:40.809 --> 00:01:43.603
And we do that,
we do that very carefully.

00:01:43.895 --> 00:01:44.979
Which means that although

00:01:44.979 --> 00:01:47.857
the endowment is quite large,
it is also quite restricted

00:01:48.149 --> 00:01:48.942
as we utilize

00:01:48.942 --> 00:01:51.861
those funds for various parts
of the university operation.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:55.323
Endowment income contributes
a third of the revenue

00:01:55.323 --> 00:01:57.617
that comprises the annual
operating budget,

00:01:58.117 --> 00:02:00.745
making it the university's
largest revenue source.

00:02:01.663 --> 00:02:04.290
Over the course of a decade,
the university spends

00:02:04.541 --> 00:02:06.751
half of the value
of the endowment.

00:02:06.751 --> 00:02:09.087 line:68%
So an endowment
is really very different

00:02:09.087 --> 00:02:10.338 line:71%
than a savings account.

00:02:10.338 --> 00:02:12.715 line:68%
If you think about a family's
savings account,

00:02:13.216 --> 00:02:15.927 line:68%
families save usually
to make a big expenditure.

00:02:15.927 --> 00:02:17.387 line:71%
So they might be saving to buy

00:02:17.387 --> 00:02:20.473
a house or a car or pay
for their children's education,

00:02:21.432 --> 00:02:23.351
or they might be saving
for an emergency,

00:02:23.351 --> 00:02:25.061
kind of an emergency fund.

00:02:25.061 --> 00:02:26.354
But ultimately,

00:02:26.354 --> 00:02:28.606
those savings are meant
to be spent at some point

00:02:28.606 --> 00:02:30.608
and that's not the case
with an endowment.

00:02:31.067 --> 00:02:33.069
An endowment is invested
in the earnings on

00:02:33.069 --> 00:02:36.573
the endowment can be spent, but
the endowment itself can't be.

00:02:36.906 --> 00:02:41.119
James Tobin developed the idea
of intergenerational equity.

00:02:41.578 --> 00:02:44.956
James Tobin was an economics
professor at Yale

00:02:44.956 --> 00:02:46.124
and a Nobel Prize winner

00:02:46.124 --> 00:02:46.666
and also

00:02:46.666 --> 00:02:49.878
my macroeconomics professor
in graduate school at Yale.

00:02:50.003 --> 00:02:53.214
And it was the notion
that an institution like Yale

00:02:53.214 --> 00:02:56.009
has an obligation
to today's students and scholars

00:02:56.009 --> 00:02:59.137
but also to maintaining
that excellence over time

00:02:59.512 --> 00:03:01.723
and making sure
that it has the resources

00:03:01.723 --> 00:03:03.766
for future scholars
and students.

00:03:04.017 --> 00:03:07.478
So by spending from the earnings
you support the mission

00:03:07.478 --> 00:03:10.899
of the institution not just
today, but in the future.

00:03:11.274 --> 00:03:14.235
[upbeat music]

00:03:15.904 --> 00:03:18.615 line:76%
The management of the endowment
is is a team effort.

00:03:19.073 --> 00:03:21.492 line:76%
Obviously,
the broader administration

00:03:21.492 --> 00:03:24.787 line:76%
and the Yale Corporation
are involved in overseeing it.

00:03:25.163 --> 00:03:27.248
But the day to day
management of the endowment

00:03:27.248 --> 00:03:29.375
is run
by the Yale Investments Office,

00:03:29.375 --> 00:03:31.336
which I'm fortunate enough to
to lead.

00:03:31.336 --> 00:03:34.047
That group of people,
which is largely made up of

00:03:34.130 --> 00:03:37.133
Yale alumni, was drawn here
for a number of reasons.

00:03:37.133 --> 00:03:39.135
I think the collegial culture,

00:03:39.135 --> 00:03:41.679
the ethically
scrupulous decision

00:03:41.679 --> 00:03:44.390
making that I think we've been
known for for a long time.

00:03:44.766 --> 00:03:46.476
And then finally,
just the ability

00:03:46.476 --> 00:03:48.728
to give back to the mission
of the institution

00:03:48.728 --> 00:03:50.355
that, again,
so many of us benefited

00:03:50.355 --> 00:03:52.649
from ourselves
as students, brought together

00:03:52.649 --> 00:03:54.943
this group of people
who run the endowment

00:03:54.943 --> 00:03:56.444
for the
benefit of the community.

00:03:56.527 --> 00:03:58.363
[sound of bike passing by]

00:03:58.363 --> 00:04:00.865
Our strategy is actually fairly
simple.

00:04:00.865 --> 00:04:02.951
It's to take a long term
approach.

00:04:03.201 --> 00:04:04.452
That's one of the core

00:04:04.452 --> 00:04:07.121
advantages we have in the market
as a multi-hundred year

00:04:07.121 --> 00:04:08.998
old institution
that expects to be around

00:04:08.998 --> 00:04:09.457
for a while

00:04:09.457 --> 00:04:12.335
still, relative to other players
who might be looking at

00:04:12.794 --> 00:04:16.172
quarter to quarter results
in the financial markets.

00:04:16.506 --> 00:04:18.549
We can take a longer term
approach and partner

00:04:18.549 --> 00:04:20.677
with some of the best investors
in the world.

00:04:20.677 --> 00:04:22.845 align:end position:100%
Over a 30 year
period, Yale's investment

00:04:22.845 --> 00:04:25.390 align:end position:100%
strategy has added over
$30 billion in value

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:28.142 align:end position:100%
relative to a passive strategy
that would allocate

00:04:28.142 --> 00:04:31.145 align:end position:100%
60% of its holdings
to stocks and 40% to bonds.

00:04:31.562 --> 00:04:32.188
Each year,

00:04:32.188 --> 00:04:33.106
Yale aims to spend

00:04:33.106 --> 00:04:35.108
five and a quarter percent
of the endowment's value,

00:04:35.483 --> 00:04:37.026
the amount
projected to be sustainable

00:04:37.026 --> 00:04:39.195
given reasonable long term
growth expectations

00:04:39.195 --> 00:04:40.905
and the endowment's
investment returns.

00:04:40.905 --> 00:04:41.948
Because long term higher

00:04:41.948 --> 00:04:44.492
education inflation averages
about 4% a year,

00:04:44.951 --> 00:04:47.036 align:end position:100% line:68%
the endowment needs to grow
by approximately

00:04:47.036 --> 00:04:48.579 align:end position:100% line:68%
nine and a quarter percent
each year,

00:04:48.579 --> 00:04:50.206 align:start size:81% position:19% line:71%
net of gifts and transfers

00:04:50.206 --> 00:04:52.208 align:end position:100% line:68%
in order to maintain
its real value

00:04:52.208 --> 00:04:54.877 align:end position:100% line:68%
while also providing operating
funds to the university.

00:04:55.336 --> 00:04:58.631 align:end position:100% line:68%
The endowment spending
policy balances two goals,

00:04:59.632 --> 00:05:02.260 align:end position:100% line:68%
and those goals are kind
of in conflict with one another.

00:05:02.844 --> 00:05:07.849 align:end position:100% line:68%
On the one hand, we want steady
funding for the programs

00:05:08.224 --> 00:05:11.644
and operations that the faculty,
staff and students depend on.

00:05:11.978 --> 00:05:15.314
And on the other hand,
eventually the spending

00:05:15.314 --> 00:05:18.818
that we have has to reflect
the resources we have.

00:05:18.818 --> 00:05:20.862
That is,
the value of the endowment.

00:05:20.862 --> 00:05:24.782
The endowment spending
rule balances those two goals

00:05:25.074 --> 00:05:31.205
by smoothing fluctuations
in the value of the endowment

00:05:31.539 --> 00:05:35.376
and slowing the incorporation
of those changes

00:05:35.376 --> 00:05:37.795
in the value of the endowment
into the budget.

00:05:38.004 --> 00:05:40.048
The way this smoothing works

00:05:40.048 --> 00:05:41.382
is that every year

00:05:41.382 --> 00:05:43.676
the spending from the endowment
is calculated

00:05:44.177 --> 00:05:46.804
and we have a target
spending rate

00:05:46.846 --> 00:05:50.516
of 5.25% of the endowment's
value.

00:05:50.933 --> 00:05:52.810
The smoothing equation
calculates

00:05:52.810 --> 00:05:56.814
20% of each year's endowment
spending by applying the target

00:05:56.814 --> 00:06:01.402
spending rate: 5.25%,
to the endowment's fiscal year

00:06:01.402 --> 00:06:04.739
end market value
from two fiscal years prior.

00:06:05.198 --> 00:06:08.701
The other 80% comes from
the previous year's spending.

00:06:09.452 --> 00:06:14.624 line:0%
The smoothing rule means that
the 40.2% return in 2021 isn't

00:06:14.624 --> 00:06:18.878 align:end position:100% line:0%
reflected as a 40.2% increase
in spending the next year.

00:06:19.670 --> 00:06:22.715 align:end position:100% line:0%
Instead, it's averaged
into the budget over time,

00:06:23.091 --> 00:06:26.010 align:end position:100% line:0%
beginning with the fiscal year
2023 budget.

00:06:26.511 --> 00:06:27.428 align:start size:31% position:69% line:0%
That year,

00:06:27.428 --> 00:06:31.641 line:0%
the endowment will provide $1.7
billion to the annual budget,

00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:35.353 align:end position:100% line:0%
an increase of
about $170 million

00:06:35.770 --> 00:06:38.856 align:end position:100% line:0%
or about 10%
over the previous year.

00:06:39.107 --> 00:06:41.317 align:end position:100% line:0%
It would also work
the other way.

00:06:41.317 --> 00:06:45.029 align:end position:100% line:0%
Say the endowment
were to experience a -25%

00:06:45.029 --> 00:06:49.158 line:0%
return one year which is what
happened in fiscal year 2009.

00:06:50.201 --> 00:06:51.786 align:end position:100% line:0%
The effect
of that return wouldn't

00:06:51.786 --> 00:06:54.122 align:end position:100% line:0%
be felt in the endowment
spending for two years

00:06:54.789 --> 00:06:58.042 line:0%
allowing the budgeting process
to make gradual adjustments

00:06:58.042 --> 00:07:00.211 align:end position:100% line:0%
rather than more
severe disruptions

00:07:00.211 --> 00:07:02.338 align:end position:100% line:0%
to the university's
core mission.

00:07:02.338 --> 00:07:03.589 align:start size:50% position:50% line:0%
And even then it

00:07:03.589 --> 00:07:07.176 line:0%
would be averaged in over time
just as positive returns are,

00:07:07.510 --> 00:07:09.637 align:end position:100% line:0%
so that endowment spending
would decrease

00:07:09.804 --> 00:07:12.306 align:end position:100% line:0%
by a much smaller
percentage in the short run

00:07:12.515 --> 00:07:14.851 align:end position:100% line:0%
than the investment
return might suggest.

00:07:15.226 --> 00:07:18.187
[inspiring music]

00:07:20.148 --> 00:07:22.817
The endowment supports
every aspect of the university,

00:07:22.817 --> 00:07:25.987
everything from scholarships
that are provided to students

00:07:25.987 --> 00:07:27.488
so they can study here

00:07:27.488 --> 00:07:29.532
to the salaries
that support the faculty

00:07:29.532 --> 00:07:31.826
and staff that work here,
you see that

00:07:31.826 --> 00:07:34.162
in the form of new buildings
that have been constructed,

00:07:34.162 --> 00:07:37.665
such as the humanities
quadrangle that recently opened.

00:07:37.748 --> 00:07:39.083
But it's not just buildings.

00:07:39.083 --> 00:07:41.335
It's also the academic side
of things.

00:07:41.335 --> 00:07:44.755
What happens in our offices,
what happens in our libraries,

00:07:44.755 --> 00:07:46.632
what happens
in our laboratories.

00:07:46.632 --> 00:07:49.677
So the idea of being able
to build a quantum computer

00:07:49.677 --> 00:07:51.762
is something that is supported
by the endowment.

00:07:52.096 --> 00:07:53.222
Finding solutions

00:07:53.222 --> 00:07:54.640
to the various aspects

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:56.976
of climate change
that are facing our planet,

00:07:56.976 --> 00:07:59.020
that is supported
by the endowment.

00:07:59.020 --> 00:08:01.063
Trying to understand
human cognition

00:08:01.063 --> 00:08:02.648
and how the brain functions.

00:08:02.648 --> 00:08:05.401
That's part of what is supported
by the endowment.

00:08:05.651 --> 00:08:07.904
So as you think about
the university,

00:08:07.904 --> 00:08:11.657
basically every aspect of it
in some form or another

00:08:11.657 --> 00:08:14.035
is supported by these
endowment funds

00:08:14.035 --> 00:08:15.703
that we have available to us.

00:08:15.995 --> 00:08:18.247
If you ask the question
how much does it actually cost

00:08:18.247 --> 00:08:20.333
to provide an undergraduate
education at Yale,

00:08:20.333 --> 00:08:23.628
the answer is it's about twice
the cost of what we charge.

00:08:24.045 --> 00:08:26.505
So even students
that are paying full price

00:08:27.215 --> 00:08:30.760
for their tuition at Yale,
and many students are not,

00:08:31.093 --> 00:08:32.887
but even those that are paying
full price

00:08:32.887 --> 00:08:34.388
are still being subsidized

00:08:34.388 --> 00:08:38.017
by about 50% relative to the
actual cost of their education.

00:08:38.601 --> 00:08:41.229
Those that are receiving
substantial financial aid are,

00:08:41.229 --> 00:08:43.147
of course, even further
subsidized.

00:08:43.147 --> 00:08:45.816 align:start size:66% position:34% line:71%
Yale has been unique.

00:08:45.816 --> 00:08:48.527 align:end position:100% line:68%
It's one of the really
handful of institutions

00:08:49.487 --> 00:08:54.200 align:end position:100% line:68%
that is able to attract students
and admit students

00:08:55.243 --> 00:08:57.536
irrespective
of their ability to pay.

00:08:58.913 --> 00:09:01.624
And so if you are a
talented person

00:09:02.291 --> 00:09:04.335
anywhere in the world
and you want to go to Yale

00:09:04.335 --> 00:09:06.295
and you can't,
your parents can't afford it.

00:09:06.295 --> 00:09:07.922
Yale will make that possible.

00:09:07.922 --> 00:09:10.466
And the endowment
is critical to that.

00:09:10.841 --> 00:09:13.219
When you think of
all the people's lives

00:09:13.261 --> 00:09:15.346
it has touched
as an institution.

00:09:15.805 --> 00:09:17.431
They've gone on
to make contributions

00:09:17.431 --> 00:09:18.808
in their community
and the world.

00:09:18.808 --> 00:09:21.185
It's an amazing thing.

00:09:21.435 --> 00:09:24.105
And what's really exciting
about the endowment today

00:09:24.105 --> 00:09:27.400
is that our plans at Yale
are so ambitious.

00:09:28.234 --> 00:09:30.945
We're trying to do things
that will

00:09:31.529 --> 00:09:34.115
really change the world
in really meaningful ways.

00:09:34.156 --> 00:09:36.575
You have brilliant people
at the institution

00:09:37.034 --> 00:09:40.621
who are motivated to tackle
some of the world's

00:09:41.163 --> 00:09:46.544
most challenging problems
and if you want to fund people

00:09:46.544 --> 00:09:48.379
to take on new challenges,

00:09:48.379 --> 00:09:50.715
you have to keep increasing
your endowment.

00:09:50.715 --> 00:09:52.216
Our donors are,

00:09:52.216 --> 00:09:55.511
you know, absolutely critical
to the future of the university.

00:09:55.720 --> 00:09:58.931
They want to fund efforts

00:09:58.931 --> 00:10:03.853
that will solve the great
mysteries of life and that will

00:10:04.186 --> 00:10:06.981
you know, really move the needle
in terms of profoundly

00:10:06.981 --> 00:10:08.482
changing the world.

00:10:08.482 --> 00:10:11.444
(Matt)
Our goal is to support
the university's mission

00:10:11.444 --> 00:10:12.737
in perpetuity.

00:10:12.737 --> 00:10:16.073
And we believe
that careful stewardship

00:10:16.073 --> 00:10:18.242
of the endowment
is critical to that mission.

00:10:18.242 --> 00:10:20.369
That's what we spend
every day here thinking about.

00:10:20.411 --> 00:10:21.704
That's what my colleagues

00:10:21.704 --> 00:10:24.999
and I wake up in the morning
coming to the office to do.

00:10:25.124 --> 00:10:27.043
And that's why we're all
here, to give back to Yale.

